/*
:name: mem_ordering_none
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: mem_ordering_none
:pyrope_top: mem_ordering_none
*/
// `ordering="none"` showcase (lhdsuite array_problem.md, ruled 2026-07-27):
// no ordering hardware at all — a read of an address written this same cycle
// is UNDEFINED (simulation: random, since sim does not model `?`; formal:
// `?`, i.e. either value is acceptable). Reads of non-written addresses
// return the stored value as usual. The golden .v encodes the undefined
// window as x, which the LEC miter treats as don't-care — so this pair
// proves for ANY refinement (today's fwd lowering included): by construction
// it can never refute an implementation, it pins only the defined
// (non-colliding) behavior and documents the mode. The user-visible payoff
// of "none" is cheapest hardware + the formal `?` that lets equivalence
// prove when collision values genuinely do not matter (in which case
// program ordering was never needed).
pub mod mem_ordering_none(clk:u1, a1:u2, we:u1, a2:u2, d2:u2, a4:u2) -> (o1:u2@[], o4:u2@[]) {
  reg mem:[4]u2:[ordering="none"] = 0
  o1 = mem[a1]
  if we != 0 {
    mem[a2] = d2
  }
  o4 = mem[a4]
}
